<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=optical-fiber">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Optical fibre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Optical fiber, Fibre optics</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Optical fibre is a flexible strand of glass that carries light and is used as a medium for telecommunication and computer networking. Daniel Colladon and Jacques Babinet showed the guiding of light by refraction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in the early 1840s. In 1953 Bram van Heel demonstrated image transmission through bundles of fibres with a transparent cladding. The first working fibre-optic data transmission system was shown by Manfred Börner at Telefunken Research Labs in Ulm in 1965. In 1970 researchers at Corning Glass Works reached an attenuation of 17 dB/km, below the 20 dB/km that Charles Kao and George Hockham had proposed in 1965 as the limit for a practical medium.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/optical-fiber.jpg" alt="Optical fibre" width="330" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BigRiz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fibreoptic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1965</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Ulm, Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Manfred Börner · Charles K. Kao · George A. Hockham</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The principle of guiding light by refraction was demonstrated in Paris in the early 1840s and image bundles with cladding in 1953; 1965 is treated as the first working data transmission and 1970 as the first fibre meeting the 20 dB/km attenuation limit.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/glassmaking">Glassmaking<span>c. 2500 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">Telephone<span>1861</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/laser">Laser<span>16 May 1960</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=optical-fiber">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
